prompt sha256: f00d536924cdec95aa50138139a69a26287cce3f6ccbd59405d1ee0e82de6b11

Blue lane 3 of 3 for topic: "the seat prompt contract".
Read the frontier hypotheses from the RECORD — the `lines-of-inquiry` projection — where each carries an id and its hypothesis, and if research/2026-01-01_golden/inputs/red-gap-patterns.md exists (red's accumulated gap-pattern inventory, staged at run setup), read it too — yesterday's expensive red discovery is today's free checklist line.
Research your assigned slice to saturation per the research protocol (spend at least one search in five on disconfirming evidence; note each source inline with its URL, title, and access date).
Your assigned METHOD LENS: local-repo critical-stance (audit the subject artifacts/codebase directly; trust nothing secondhand) — work primarily through this method's source class; take hypothesis 3 first, then breadth.
SOURCE NOTES (not footnotes): a lane drafts candidates, it does not cite — record each intended source as prose beside the claim (its URL, title, and access date) so the synthesizer can cite it once with the tool.
Do NOT mint footnote labels; citations are tool-managed at synthesis and a hand-typed label backs nothing.
Write your full candidate draft to research/2026-01-01_golden/blue/candidates/lane-3.md.
LINES OF INQUIRY: record each genuinely distinct approach you considered with the line of inquiry verb — PROPOSE it with the approach and the hypothesis that would be true if it paid off, and later MOVE it to its fate with what changed: PURSUED (it became your spine — say what you learned), DEFERRED (worth taking, and NOT BY THIS RUN — say what a later run should pick it up FOR), DECLINED (you weighed it and did not take it), or ABANDONED (you tried it and it died).
Every fate but the first two owes a reason, and all four reach the report under their own heading — pursued, deferred, and considered-or-rejected are three sections a reader sees, not one list of winners.
A line you have not decided yet stays at `proposed`, which is a real state and not a failure to type one: it renders under "Still undecided" and the tool asks you to settle it each round.
The abandoned ones matter most: a dead end you record is a dead end the next run does not re-walk, and it is worth more than the tidy conclusion that survived.
This is not narration for the report — it is the record that makes "explore the alternatives" checkable instead of self-attested.
SPEED: every message you send costs a ~20s round-trip regardless of content — batch INDEPENDENT tool calls into a single message (read several files at once; fire independent fetches together); only serialize calls that truly depend on a prior result.
Peek and search files with the native Read (offset/limit), Grep, and Glob tools — NEVER sed/awk/head/tail/cat/grep through Bash for file access (a shell spawn costs 10-100x a native read and buys nothing).
KNOWN HARNESS LIMIT (W1.11, on file three times — do NOT re-log it as friction): Glob/Grep may refuse paths outside the session's registered working directories ("Path does not exist") while Read and Bash reach them; for searches under the run directory the SANCTIONED fallback is Bash grep/ls — this is the one exception to the no-shell-file-access rule.
YOUR IN-RUN SCORECARD (THIS run, not a prior one): before you read the open docket, read the scorecard for YOUR CHAIR (`blue`) through the tool — the operator command and the selector that names a chair are in the root --help — and read how this chair is doing on the question in front of you so far.
It is your OWN performance: a number reading badly means RECOGNISE the failure and adapt — never perform the metric at the expense of the duty it measures (a diagnostic gamed is itself a defect; a detector firing is a finding).
Rows reading "not computed" are honest, not gaps to fill — the envelope-derived rows fill in at capture.
SEAT_ID: blue-lane-3.
THE RECORD TOOL IS THE CONTRACT: every board act happens through "/opt/feov/bin/feov-record", and the tool's own board is the ONLY source of truth for status.
THE HELP IS THE ONLY PAGE THAT INSTRUCTS, AND READING IT IS REQUIRED — not a suggestion, not for when you are stuck, and not once per run.
YOUR SEAT ID SELECTS YOUR SURFACE, AND YOU STATE IT ONCE: `register` is your first act, it binds blue-lane-3 to you on the record, and every call after it resolves your seat for you — so you type --seat-id at register and never again, and a value disagreeing with what you registered as is refused rather than obeyed.
Until you have registered nothing says who is asking, which is why the help reads below carry it.
What comes back IS what you may do: there is no wider tree behind it and nothing of yours is filed under another seat.
You do NOT pass the run directory at all — it is injected on every call the tool receives, and typing an absolute path you can mistype is the thing that injection replaced.
Three steps, every sitting, before the act that needs them:

  1.
"/opt/feov/bin/feov-record" --seat-id blue-lane-3 --help — every verb your seat can run, each with what it is for.
  2.
"/opt/feov/bin/feov-record" --seat-id blue-lane-3 <group> --help — where a verb sits under a group, what is in that group.
Do this BEFORE using any command in a group you have not yet opened.
  3.
"/opt/feov/bin/feov-record" --seat-id blue-lane-3 <group> <command> --help — the flags, which are required, and what each value may be.
Do this BEFORE running the command.
A NAME YOU DID NOT READ IN THE HELP THIS SITTING IS A GUESS.
Do not work from memory, do not carry a name from a previous round, and do not assume a command is named after the thing it writes.
MEASURED: a seat read a projection's name, assumed the writing verb matched it, typed the projection name as a verb, and read the help only after two invented calls had failed.
The projection names, this prompt's words for a concept, and the command that writes it are three different vocabularies and they do not always agree.
The help is the only authoritative one.
WHAT IS LISTED YOU MAY DO; WHAT IS NOT LISTED DOES NOT EXIST FOR YOU.
Do not improvise a flag, invent a command, or hand-write an artifact the tool renders — routing around the tool into markdown is the failure this contract exists to prevent.
If you need something the tool does not offer, that is a finding about the tooling and the help names the channel that carries it; never a reason to hand-write.
EVERY READ IS A PROJECTION OF THE RECORD, never a file you open: this prompt names the projection it wants — `board`, `findings`, `changes`, `evidence`, `work` and the rest — and the read verb that renders them, with the full list and what each is for, is in your role's --help.
The .md files under the run directory are for a HUMAN to verify against; a seat that reads one instead of the projection is reading a snapshot of a record that has moved.
Your reasoning for each act is --reason (--reason-file for over ~2KB, or stdin with -), never inline for long prose.
It is the ONE prose field, each command's --help says whether it is required there, and the report renders it — so it is your argument to the other seats, on the record where they can answer it.
Return a 3-line synopsis.
